From f883635875868a505f7311c996a6e076f788747c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 10:16:31 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [DOSKUTSU] patch 0304 -- measure the engine bucket on the
 fixed-timestep path

Every engine-side timing instrument in the tree is anchored to
Game::tick(), and main.cpp:1823 routes GM_NORMAL to run_tick_fixed()
whenever fixed_timestep_active() -- default-ON since 0159. So during the
gameplay the campaign actually measures, no engine timer is bumped at
all: the engine half of the `audio/engine split block` line reads a flat
zero, and the audit early-returns entirely unless an Organya stream
callback keeps the audio counter alive.

Round-G logs show it directly. The three OPL3 cells emit 20-22
phase-timing blocks and exactly 2 split blocks -- title/menu, on the 1:1
path -- then go dark. The Organya cell emits 20 split blocks reading
engine_tick_calls=99, 76, then 0 for every remaining block while
audio_mixer_calls climbs to 240.

FINE_INSTR was no escape hatch either. Its sub-bucket timers do run
inside tick_state/tick_render_submit, but the emit is gated on
g_eng_residual_idx > 0 and the only push site is inside Game::tick, so
the block never printed on the fixed path however the flags were set.

The consequence is that the audio+engine bucket -- ~68% of the frame on
the Mach64, and the largest single bucket on both cards -- has never
been decomposed on the path that runs. What can be read today says the
label is misleading: the one measured audio component (Organya PCM mix)
is ~2.0 ms/flip against a derived bucket of ~34-56 ms/flip, about 5%.

Bracket both halves of the fixed tick so the existing split audit
reports an engine bucket during gameplay, with no change to the audit
itself and none to Renderer.cpp.

Gated on FINE_INSTR, default OFF, where the 1:1 path's EngineTickTimer
is unconditional. This bracket lands on the hot path every measured cell
runs, and the Round-G VIDM/VIDMI arms put instrumentation cost at ~0.6
fps of ~14 (~4%) on a 486DX2-66; leaving it always-on would tax the very
numbers the campaign is attributing, with an unbisected ~4% round-2 ->
round-3 regression still open. FINE_INSTR rather than a new hint because
it already gates the sub-buckets inside these two functions, so parent
and children now switch on together.

Double-count guard, and the reason it is needed: Game::tick() is a thin
WRAPPER that calls tick_state() then tick_render_submit(), with only
instrumentation resets in between. Without a guard the ungated parent
timer and both new child timers would accumulate into the same counter
on the 1:1 path, reporting ~2x true engine time and corrupting the
title/menu bucket -- the one place the instrument previously worked.
Game::tick now claims ownership (RAII, so an early exit cannot leave the
flag stuck set and silently blind the fixed path) and the child timers
stand down while it is held. Deleting the parent instead would be
simpler and is wrong: it is ungated, so removing it would drop the
engine bucket from default FINE_INSTR=0 runs. Guard storage lives in
game.cpp, not Renderer.cpp, so this patch touches no file another
workstream is editing.

Witnessed under DOSBox-X: with the guard, per-block engine_tick_calls
equals the Game::tick residual sample count exactly (99/99, then 100/100
across ten consecutive blocks), which is what single-counting predicts
and is impossible under the double-count. Default gameplay banner gate
PASSES. The fixed-path bucket itself is NOT yet witnessed -- the DOSBox
smoke never reaches sustained unpaused GM_NORMAL, so that still needs a
real-HW cell with FINE_INSTR=1.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/Utils/diagnostic_timers.h | 60 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 src/game.cpp                  | 78 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 136 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/Utils/diagnostic_timers.h b/src/Utils/diagnostic_timers.h
index c8cde8c3..e3aa56ee 100644
--- a/src/Utils/diagnostic_timers.h
+++ b/src/Utils/diagnostic_timers.h
@@ -115,6 +115,66 @@ struct EngineTickTimer
 // stay decoupled from the Renderer class.
 bool engine_tick_fine_instr_active();
 
+// DOS-PORT (patch 0304): FINE_INSTR-gated sibling of EngineTickTimer,
+// for the FIXED-TIMESTEP path (Game::tick_state / tick_render_submit).
+//
+// Feeds the SAME g_engine_tick_* counters as EngineTickTimer, so the
+// `audio/engine split block` line needs no change to report an engine
+// bucket during GM_NORMAL gameplay -- which it previously could not,
+// because EngineTickTimer sits only in Game::tick() and main.cpp routes
+// GM_NORMAL to run_tick_fixed() instead. See the rationale block at the
+// top of Game::tick_state().
+//
+// Gated where EngineTickTimer is not, and deliberately so: this bracket
+// lands on the hot path that every measured cell runs, and the Round-G
+// VIDM/VIDMI arms put instrumentation cost at ~0.6 fps of ~14 (~4%) on
+// a 486DX2-66. Leaving it always-on would tax the very numbers the
+// campaign is trying to attribute, with an unbisected ~4% round-2 ->
+// round-3 regression already open. Default OFF = default binary
+// unchanged. FINE_INSTR is the right existing flag rather than a new
+// hint: it already gates the engine sub-buckets that live inside these
+// two functions, so one flag now turns on the parent bucket and its
+// children together instead of leaving the parent unmeasurable.
+//
+// `on` is captured once at construction; when false both the ctor and
+// dtor skip the clock reads entirely and the cost is one cached
+// predicate per call.
+//
+// DOUBLE-COUNT GUARD. `Game::tick()` is a thin WRAPPER -- it calls
+// tick_state() then tick_render_submit(), with only instrumentation
+// resets in between (game.cpp:314-315). So on the 1:1 path the
+// pre-existing EngineTickTimer (whole body) and both of these timers
+// (each half) would accumulate into the SAME counter, reading ~2x true
+// engine time and corrupting the title/menu bucket -- the one place the
+// instrument previously worked. `g_engine_tick_parent_active` is set by
+// Game::tick around its delegation; while it is set these timers stand
+// down entirely and the parent owns the measurement.
+//
+// Deleting the parent instead would be simpler and is wrong: it is
+// ungated, so removing it would drop the engine bucket from default
+// (FINE_INSTR=0) runs. Storage is defined in game.cpp, not Renderer.cpp.
+extern bool g_engine_tick_parent_active;
+
+struct FixedPathEngineTickTimer
+{
+  const bool on;
+  std::uint64_t t0;
+  FixedPathEngineTickTimer()
+      : on(!g_engine_tick_parent_active && engine_tick_fine_instr_active()),
+        t0(on ? SDL_GetTicksNS() : 0)
+  {
+  }
+  ~FixedPathEngineTickTimer()
+  {
+    if (!on) return;
+    const std::uint64_t dt = SDL_GetTicksNS() - t0;
+    g_engine_tick_accum_ns_block += dt;
+    g_engine_tick_accum_ns_global += dt;
+    ++g_engine_tick_count_block;
+    ++g_engine_tick_count_global;
+  }
+};
+
 // DOS-PORT (patch 0160, SFX-stall instrumentation contract -- part A):
 // per-SFX-trigger marker. sfx_trigger_record() is the NXE::Diag-facing
 // forwarder (defined at the end of Renderer.cpp, mirroring the
diff --git a/src/game.cpp b/src/game.cpp
index f5213912..0d94c956 100644
--- a/src/game.cpp
+++ b/src/game.cpp
@@ -221,6 +221,20 @@ bool Game::pause(int pausemode, int param)
   return 0;
 }
 
+// DOS-PORT (patch 0304): storage for the engine_tick double-count guard
+// declared in Utils/diagnostic_timers.h. Defined HERE rather than in
+// Renderer.cpp (where the sibling g_engine_tick_* counters live) so this
+// patch touches no file another workstream is editing. Single-threaded
+// by construction -- the engine spawns no threads on the DOS port, and
+// the SDL3 DOS backend's scheduler is cooperative.
+namespace NXE
+{
+namespace Diag
+{
+bool g_engine_tick_parent_active = false;
+}
+}
+
 void Game::tick(void)
 {
   // patch 0062 (wave 13.1-D2): time the entire engine-tick body. Sibling
@@ -311,8 +325,24 @@ void Game::tick(void)
   // (was here at top-of-tick, now inside tick_render_submit after the
   // logic; behavior-preserving because logic never reads the
   // framebuffer). See PHASE11-WAVE-56-CONTRACT.md.
-  tick_state();
-  tick_render_submit();
+  // DOS-PORT (patch 0304): claim ownership of the engine_tick counters
+  // for the duration of the delegation. Game::tick is a WRAPPER around
+  // these two calls, so without this the ungated EngineTickTimer above
+  // and the FINE_INSTR-gated FixedPathEngineTickTimer inside each half
+  // would all accumulate into the same counter and report ~2x true
+  // engine time on this path. RAII so an early exit out of either half
+  // cannot leave the flag stuck set (which would silently blind the
+  // fixed path for the rest of the process).
+  {
+    struct _ParentClaim
+    {
+      _ParentClaim()  { NXE::Diag::g_engine_tick_parent_active = true;  }
+      ~_ParentClaim() { NXE::Diag::g_engine_tick_parent_active = false; }
+    } _pc;
+
+    tick_state();
+    tick_render_submit();
+  }
 
   // patch 0152 (wave-56 B1 -- mode_tick split, refinement B opt iv):
   // the W20B mode_tick parent total = the state slice + the render
@@ -471,6 +501,40 @@ bool menu_slide_ft_active()
 // foreclosure of the wave-36 -28 fps drawcall-coupling trap.
 void Game::tick_state(void)
 {
+  // DOS-PORT (patch 0304): engine-tick accounting on the FIXED-TIMESTEP
+  // path. EngineTickTimer used to sit only at the top of Game::tick(),
+  // which main.cpp's dispatch (`fixed_timestep_active() && game.mode ==
+  // GM_NORMAL` -> run_tick_fixed) never calls. FIXED_TIMESTEP has been
+  // default-ON since 0159, so throughout GM_NORMAL gameplay the engine
+  // half of the `audio/engine split block` line read a flat zero and the
+  // audit early-returned entirely unless an Organya stream callback kept
+  // the audio counter alive. Round-G evidence: the three OPL3 cells emit
+  // 20-22 phase-timing blocks and exactly 2 split blocks (title/menu, on
+  // the 1:1 path); the Organya cell emits 20 split blocks with
+  // engine_tick_calls=99, 76, then 0 for every remaining block. The
+  // ~68%-of-frame "audio+engine" bucket was therefore never decomposed
+  // on the path that actually runs.
+  //
+  // Bracketing BOTH halves keeps the bucket's meaning identical to the
+  // 1:1 path -- sibling costs in the caller (input_poll, runFade) stay
+  // outside, exactly as diagnostic_timers.h documents for Game::tick,
+  // and the drawcalls submitted by the render half are inside on both
+  // paths (Game::tick drew too), so `engine_tick_ms_x10` overlaps
+  // dc_total by design. Subtract dc_total for non-drawcall engine time;
+  // the derived `audio+engine_ms` remainder is computed from dc_total
+  // and flip_inner independently, so nothing there changes.
+  //
+  // Read `engine_tick_ms_x10` (per-flip ms, accum/flips) as the engine
+  // bucket. Do NOT read `engine_tick_calls` or the per-call us figure
+  // across paths: here the count is (catch-up tick_state runs + 1
+  // render submit) per displayed frame rather than 1, so per-call is
+  // comparable only within the fixed path.
+  //
+  // FINE_INSTR-gated, default OFF -- see FixedPathEngineTickTimer in
+  // diagnostic_timers.h for why this one is gated where the 1:1 path's
+  // EngineTickTimer is not.
+  NXE::Diag::FixedPathEngineTickTimer _ett;
+
   debug_clear();
 
   if (game.paused)
@@ -561,6 +625,16 @@ void Game::tick_state(void)
 // holds -- contract sec.1.3).
 void Game::tick_render_submit(void)
 {
+  // DOS-PORT (patch 0304): the render half of the same bracket -- see
+  // the rationale block at the top of tick_state(). Placed OUTSIDE the
+  // ClearScreenTimer scope below so the two nest cleanly (this timer is
+  // the parent bucket; ClearScreenTimer / ModeTickRenderTimer /
+  // DebugOverlayTimer are its FINE_INSTR sub-buckets, and sub-buckets
+  // are meant to sum to at most the parent). Sharing the FINE_INSTR
+  // gate with those sub-buckets means the whole engine layer -- parent
+  // and children -- switches on together.
+  NXE::Diag::FixedPathEngineTickTimer _ett;
+
   {
     NXE::Diag::ClearScreenTimer _cst;
     Renderer::getInstance()->clearScreen(BLACK);
-- 
